<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Taylor Series Expansion and Application</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Determine the Taylor series for \(f(x)={@fx@}\) about \(x={@xx@}\). </p>
 <p>We proceed by steps </p>
<p>Let \(f(x)={@fx@}\)</p>
<p><b>Step </b> \(1:\) Compute the derivatives of \(f(x)\) up to order \(4\) and evaluate each derivative at \(x={@xx@}.\) </p>
<p>\(f^{(1) }(x)=\) [[input:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]] \(\qquad \implies f^{(1)}({@xx@})=\) [[input:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]][[validation:ans1]][[validation:ans2]]</p>
<p>\(f^{(2) }(x)=\) [[input:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]] \(\qquad \implies f^{(2)}({@xx@})=\) [[input:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]][[validation:ans3]][[validation:ans4]]</p>
<p>\(f^{(3) }(x)=\) [[input:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]] \(\qquad \implies f^{(3)}({@xx@})=\) [[input:ans6]] [[feedback:prt6]][[validation:ans5]][[validation:ans6]]</p>
<p>\(f^{(4) }(x)=\) [[input:ans7]] [[feedback:prt7]] \(\qquad \implies f^{(4)}({@xx@})=\) [[input:ans8]] [[feedback:prt8]][[validation:ans7]][[validation:ans8]]</p>
<p><b>Step</b> \(2:\) Give the summation of the first \(4\) terms of the Taylor series of \(f(x)={@fx@}\).
</p>
<p> [[input:ans9]] [[validation:ans9]][[feedback:prt9]]</p>

<p><b>Step</b> \(3:\) Use the Taylor Series formula of \(e^{x}\) to determine the Taylor Series for \(f(x)={@fx@}\) about \(x={@xx@}\)</p>
<p>Hint: \( \sum_{n=k}^{\infty} e^{x} \) is typed as <code> sum(e^(x),n,k,inf) </code></p>
<p>\( \quad \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{f^n({@xx@})}{n!}({@x-xx@})^n=\) [[input:ans10]] [[validation:ans10]][[feedback:prt10]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3> Worked Solution</h3>
<p><b>Step </b> \(1:\) Compute the derivatives of \(f(x)\) up to order \( 4 \) and evaluate each derivative at \(x={@xx@}.\) </p>
<p>Since \( f(x) = {@fx@} \)
<p>
\(
\begin{align}
n=1: \qquad f^{(1)}(x) &= {@f1@}  \\
n=2: \qquad f^{(2)}(x) &= {@f2@}  \\
n=3:  \qquad f^{(3)}(x) &= {@f3@}  \\
n=4: \qquad f^{(4)}(x) &= {@f4@}  \\
 \end{align}
\)
</p>

<p> Evaluate each derivative at \(x = {@xx@}\) to find the coefficients of the Taylor series.
</p>
<p>\(f^{(1) }({@xx@})={@f1x@}\) </p>
<p>\(f^{ (2) }({@xx@})={@f2x@}\) </p>
<p>\(f^{ (3) }({@xx@})={@f3x@}\) </p>
<p>\(f^{ (4) } ({@xx@})={@f4x@}\) </p>

<p><b>Step</b> \(2:\) Use the Taylor Series to compute the summation of the first \(4\) terms for \(f(x)={@fx@}\) about \(x={@xx@}\)</p>
<p>We recall the general formula.</p>
<p>\( \quad  f(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{f^n(a)}{n!}(x-a)^n\), we have</p>
<p>The summation of the first \(4\) terms of the Taylor Series about \(x={@xx@}\)  means to find the Taylor series for \(f(x)\)  about \(x={@xx@}\) up to order \(3\) </p>
<p>\(f(x)=f({@xx@})+f^{(1)}({@xx@}) ({@x-xx@})+\frac{f^{(2)}({@xx@})}{2!} ({@x-xx@})^2+\frac{f^{(3)}({@xx@})}{3!} ({@x-xx@})^3\)</p>
<p>\( \quad  f(x)={@ta1@}\)</p>

<p><b>Step</b> \(3:\) Use the Taylor Series formula of \(e^{x}\) to determine the Taylor Series for \(f(x)={@fx@}\) about \(x={@xx@}\)</p>
<p>We recall \(e^{x}= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{f^n({@xx@})}{n!}({@x-xx@})^n\) </p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p>\( \quad f(x)={@ta2@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
